b/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132894 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132894 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "16a5fed7-59bc-5485-b040-90f121190eda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132895 #edb/ref 1080 "II, III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132895 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132895 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e50f37f-de04-5f77-ba2f-0ee2ac1b3b70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132896 #edb/ref 1080 "III, II" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132896 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132896 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e97b8ed1-34bd-5695-ad22-e12a6cc61fba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132897 #edb/ref 1080 "I, III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132897 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132897 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fa53d2ea-c1bd-5aa5-965f-9513e9eaafc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132898 #edb/ref 1080 "II, I" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132898 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132898 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d7c910c-3f13-51d6-bee7-ce0c300dfe90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132899 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132899 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186132899 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3a9192d3-9f25-57bd-a508-793119bd9a59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133477 #edb/ref 1069 "e-114" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133477 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-3)^2+2.$ According to the intermediate value theorem, for which values of $k$ does the equation $f(x)=k$ have a guaranteed solution on the interval $[2,6]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133477 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb373a5d-aa71-58ed-82e3-b74a8f51d144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133477 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by plotting the function.\n\n![](images/02/02c989a33b6f027c0814d39d1468379dcb24f90536257b2b0bf4031191a73f85.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[2,6],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(2)$ and $f(6).$\n\nSince $f(2)=3$ and $f(6)=11$, we must have \n$k\\in[3,11].$\n\n**Watch out!** According to the graph of this particular function, $f(x)=k$ may have solutions for $k < 3,$ but these are *not* guaranteed by the intermediate value theorem. The theorem only guarantees the solutions for $k$ between $f(a)$ and $f(b).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133478 #edb/ref 1069 "e-115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133478 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in[-2,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=-2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133478 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d048aae2-5773-5da7-ab18-35d41bfbb53a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133478 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-2,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-2,2].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-2,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-2},{\\color{blue}-1}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}-2})={\\color{red}4}$ and $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}.$ Since $-2 \\not \\in [{\\color{red}-1},{\\color{red}4}],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}-1},{\\color{blue}0}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}-1})={\\color{red}-1}$ and $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}.$ Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}-1}],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}0},{\\color{blue}1}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}0})={\\color{red}-3}$ and $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}.$ Since $-2 \\in [{\\color{red}-3},{\\color{red}5}],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[{\\color{blue}1},{\\color{blue}2}],$ we have $g({\\color{blue}1})={\\color{red}5}$ and $g({\\color{blue}2})={\\color{red}3}.$ Since $-2 \\not \\in[{\\color{red}3},{\\color{red}5}],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=-2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=-2$ on the subintervals $[-1,0]$ and $[0,1].$ Therefore, $g(x)=-2$ has at least two solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133481 #edb/ref 1069 "e-116" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133481 #edb/ref 1071 "On which type of closed intervals can  the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function \n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x^2+1}{x^2-1} \\, ?\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89750383-d5a6-5bf4-b6b2-abd489e44d3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133481 #edb/ref 1074 "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x^2-1=0,$ which corresponds to $x=1$ or $x=-1.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain points $x=1$ or $x=-1.$ \n\nFor instance, the intervals $[2,4]$ or $[-0.5,0.5]$ are allowed, but the intervals $[-2,0]$ and $[0.5, 1.5]$ are not." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133647 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133647 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c7/c79e9567742a64066dfe42651f3a4e05847bd5c8b7d8ee616238010a2926c087.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ above is defined and continuous on the closed interval $x\\in [-5,2].$ By filling the blank spaces in the sentence below, complete the statement of the intermediate value theorem:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $\\textrm{ ________ }$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133647 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e32fb82c-2e8f-5383-b15a-8f98aa86b716" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133647 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-5,2],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-5)$ and $f(2).$\n\nSince $f(-5)=1$ and $f(2)=4$, we must have $k\\in[1,4].$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is \"$1$ and $4$\". The complete sentence is:\n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $1$ and $4$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in [-5,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133832 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133832 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133832 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133832 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133832 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133832 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133832 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133832 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133832 #edb/ref 1039 "Completing a Statement On the Intermediate Value Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133832 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133832 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7d01d143-68f2-534a-8be6-4f63f9ab0d40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186397801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133955 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133955 #edb/ref 1039 "Selecting a Specific Value For Which a Solution Is Guaranteed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133955 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133955 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6388d315-340b-56a0-bc93-e380bfbf6ed3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133956 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133956 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Number of Solutions Given a Table of Values of a Continuous Function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133956 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133956 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "7dffa987-022c-52a2-bc21-cdace7455eab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133957 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186051076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186241118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186398834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133957 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Intervals On Which the IVT Can Be Applied" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133957 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133957 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "1012ae9a-26d6-50bf-a46a-7c859d856752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1045 "The Intermediate Value Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186134019 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "66b245e8-8d46-5eb6-a9cb-721c5323145e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134471 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134471 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47013" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134471 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = x^{2} + 4$. For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x) = k$ has a solution on the interval $x ∈ [2, 5]$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134471 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b140ca1-518c-5294-933a-fe5b97f8ee4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134472 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134472 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47019" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134472 #edb/ref 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x) = \\frac{x}{x - 1}$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134472 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7de45808-287e-58b6-b2fd-45e80ad721a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134474 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134474 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47058" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134474 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134474 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=(x+1)^{2}-4.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-4,-2]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134474 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f019924-a46f-5bc3-be22-35c5680cdf03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134474 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-4,-2],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-4)$ and $f(-2).$\n\nSince $f(-4)=5$ and $f(-2)=-3,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-3,5].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=2$ belongs to the interval $[-3,5].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134476 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134476 #edb/ref 1069 "q-48343" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134476 #edb/ref 1071 "A statement of the intermediate value theorem is given below but some of its parts are missing.\n\n> *Let $f(x)$ be a function that is $______$ on a closed interval $[a, b]$. If $k$ is a number that lies between $_________$, then there exists a number $c ∈ [a, b]$ such that $f(c) = k$.*\n\nGoing from left to right, in which order should the parts I-III listed below be placed into the blank spaces to complete the statement of the theorem?\n\n1. increasing\n2. continuous\n3. $f(a)$ and $f(b)$ inclusively" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134476 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "69dfc1c5-3195-5cf2-bc4a-740b775a4bef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134752 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134752 #edb/ref 1061 24962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134752 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e944fcc1-fe2f-5c5d-9768-0e36d6d884b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134752 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134975 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134975 #edb/ref 1061 564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134975 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8f1b0d3-494f-537b-8e99-af2aca4fc4f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134975 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134976 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134976 #edb/ref 1061 565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134976 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ce1aa6a3-c0f2-525f-a909-c241964d206f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134976 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134977 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134977 #edb/ref 1061 568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134977 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "804af12a-0f60-55f9-a36a-0d40e8fd270b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134977 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134978 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134978 #edb/ref 1061 570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134978 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec27384a-d1f9-523f-b3ef-d8bed946e5e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134978 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134979 #edb/ref 1061 571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134979 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "79d4ad5d-f388-5fc5-a8b7-a72f14369138" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134979 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135022 #edb/ref 1064 "The **intermediate value theorem (IVT)** states that:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is continuous on $\\color{blue}[a,b],$ and $k$ is a number between $\\color{red}{f(a)}$ and $\\color{red}{f(b)}$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c \\in \\color{blue}{[a,b]}$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nThe intuition behind the IVT is that if a function is continuous between two inputs, then between those two inputs, we must be able to draw the function without picking up our pen off the page, so the function must cover all values between the two corresponding outputs.\n\nThe IVT is often used to prove that some equation has a solution. For example, if we want to prove that there is a solution to $f(x)=3,$ and we know that $f(x)$ is a continuous function with $f({\\color{blue}{1}})={\\color{red}{2}}$ and $f({\\color{blue}{6}})={\\color{red}{6}},$ then the IVT tells us that there exists some solution $c \\in \\color{blue}{[1,6]}$ such that $f(c)=3.$\n\nWe can gain further intuition by visualizing the situation. First, let's plot what $y=f(x)$ *might* look like.\n\n![](images/98/9896ea1c3d3d7c461e78ce4b4b232ce1f13f329468cd28b2d8e743132de093df.png)\n\nFor this particular $f(x)$ there is one solution to $f(x)=3$, which is $x\\approx 2.$ Note that our solution lies inside the interval $\\color{blue}[1,6].$\n\nNow let's consider another *possible* $f(x).$\n\n![](images/de/dea5683d5b18bccf9623fb6a107e8ef818ddd1cef4adbef08be471869791ec27.png)\n\nThis time, there are three solutions to $f(x)=3$ which lie in the interval $\\color{blue}[1,6].$ \n\nWe can keep changing our $f(x),$ but no matter how we draw it, we will always have some solution to $f(x)=3$ which lies in the interval $\\color{blue}[1,6].$\n\n![](images/4d/4d4aaec84baff10a48f715eeae7e06af21beccd29b38c42b3283032ef0726fec.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135022 #edb/ref 1065 306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135022 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9dc98e46-26fd-5ae1-b12b-05da48928cc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135022 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135023 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the function $f(x)$ given below.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x+5, & 1\\le x<3 \\\\ -x+4, & 3\\le x<4\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCan we apply the intermediate value theorem to $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,4]?$\n\nRemember, the intermediate value theorem assumes that the function is continuous. Here, the function $f(x)$ not continuous on $[1,4].$ So, we cannot apply the intermediate value theorem.\n\nIf we plot the function, we can get an idea of why the intermediate value theorem cannot be applied.\n\n![](images/9b/9b9543ec11b350cea4ca18e24a8db6ae8f72ffc38e64f973f9af2f83f1226400.png)\n\nThe graph shows that, for example, the equation $f(x)=1.5$ has no solution on $[1,4]$ despite the fact that $1.5$ lies between $f(1)=4$ and $f(4)=0.$\nSo, the intermediate value theorem does not hold.\n\n**Watch out!** The interval on which we apply the intermediate value theorem must *always* be closed. For example, we can't apply the theorem on an interval like $[1,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135023 #edb/ref 1065 309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135023 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a6571cd4-d390-5e75-907d-726b500e6976" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135023 #edb/ref 1067 "Why Does the Function Have to Be Continuous?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238885 #edb/ref 1080 "(-2,1]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238885 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238885 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5f6a053c-5f1b-5cdf-8aca-963b7ac607b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239079 #edb/ref 1080 "[-5,-3]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239079 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239079 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "76c89a10-b5c7-5e4d-af46-660055d3e634" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239190 #edb/ref 1080 "[-8,-3)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239190 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239190 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "829ab2da-3a5d-5bbb-9052-cd31b64cc806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239789 #edb/ref 1080 "[-4,-2]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239789 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239789 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c578ab7d-a573-5c0b-98db-a13e100e72e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240160 #edb/ref 1080 "[-1,3]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240160 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240160 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eeb73c74-a9c9-5b6d-a630-7db64dffc37d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240721 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186240160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240721 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240721 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240721 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186240160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240721 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "de800b12-b7a8-5928-99a5-4ec2e10ac47d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241118 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241118 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241118 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241118 #edb/ref 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+5x+6}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241118 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12db1f60-25f5-507f-bdcf-3245dfde49c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186241118 #edb/ref 1074 "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which makes the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have ${x}^{2}+5x+6=0,$ which corresponds to $x=-3$ or $x=-2.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the points $x=-3$ or $x=-2.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-1,3]$ is allowed. The intervals $[-8,-3)$ and $(-2,1]$ are not closed, while $[-5,-3]$ and $[-4,-2]$ contain the point $x=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315135 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315135 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186315135 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "033434ee-d7b1-53a9-b9fc-d349d23e00e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316675 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316675 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316675 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316675 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186315135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186316675 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3664d67e-9a2f-5ba7-8e3c-e75c2710d2ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186316675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317540 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317540 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317540 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $p(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-6,5].$ Some values of $p(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $p(x)$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $p(x)=2?$\n\nThe equation $p(x)=2$ has at least {{field-1}} solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317540 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f368f3a-06db-598d-8b7b-f68ccf3faca3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317540 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $p$ is a continuous function on $[-6,5],$ we can apply the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT) in each subinterval of $[-6,5].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-6,5]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-6,-2],$ we have $p(-6)=4$ and $p(-2)=-1.$ Since $2\\in [-1,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-2,5],$ we have $p(-2)=-1$ and $p(5)=-3.$ Since $2\\notin [-3,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $p(x)=2$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $p(x)=2$ on $[-6,-2].$  \n\nTherefore, $p(x)=2$ has at least $1$ solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327086 #edb/ref 1080 "k=-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327086 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327086 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11904119-a193-52e5-a726-77435d9078cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327511 #edb/ref 1080 "k=10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327511 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327511 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "41a35ce7-fd1e-5a3c-98e9-33226a993fd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327675 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327675 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327675 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "55a6dd30-b257-5143-908c-94806b79918b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327709 #edb/ref 1080 "k=2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327709 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186327709 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5889f12a-e473-54e7-998d-9fe3253e5ac1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328144 #edb/ref 1080 "k=-5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328144 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328144 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8cce18a1-ac0d-5ee0-81d8-59ec1211d61e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328466 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328466 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328466 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b0a11625-83a7-523d-aa62-5f659127b27b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328585 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328585 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328585 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bd4cccbe-c232-5969-94c3-cc1c0827b4bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328989 #edb/ref 1080 "k=12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328989 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328989 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eb0487d1-7626-5782-bf7f-dc13ed94d563" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328993 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328993 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186328993 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eb627d11-6b89-5be3-8bb0-8db63422debb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329111 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329111 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329111 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f85501cf-6174-511f-b788-63273ac0be7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329236 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329236 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329236 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186327709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329236 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1f8a1662-58bc-58f6-bb48-4c6b4004daa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186327675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186328993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329362 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186329111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329362 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329362 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329362 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186329111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329362 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6551fdd8-34e7-54c6-aa84-f41b0b354cf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329803 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468507 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329803 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468508 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329803 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468509 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329803 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329803 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329803 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}.$ In which of the following intervals does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that the equation $f(x)=3$ has a solution? \n- **I.** $[-4,-1]$\n- **II.** $[3,5]$\n- **III.** $[-1,4]$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186329803 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ee052f2c-7f8b-5e1c-a710-1b1e4c2963cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329803 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{x-2}$ is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero. Therefore, $f(x)$ is discontinuous at $x=2,$ and so the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=2$ \n\nLet's make a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the endpoints.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0$ | $4$ | $2.5$ | $2$ |\n\n- On the interval $[-4,-1],$ we have $f(-4)=0.5$ and $f(-1)=0.$ Since $3\\notin [0,0.5],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- On the interval $[3,5],$ we have $f(3)=4$ and $f(5)=2.$ Since $3\\in [2,4],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $f(x)=3$ on this interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,4]$ contains the point $x=2.$ Therefore we can not apply the intermediate value theorem on this interval.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II  only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329804 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186329362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329804 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47056" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329804 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329804 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-4,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-5$ | $2$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329804 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7dd8dfeb-1c7c-5f8b-910b-7d58a3bb506e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186329804 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-4,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-4,2].$\n\nSo, we will go through each subinterval of $[-4,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1.$\n\n- On the subinterval $[-4,-3],$ we have $g(-4)=2$ and $g(-3)=-1.$ Since $1\\in [-1,2],$  the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-3,-1],$ we have $g(-3)=-1$ and $g(-1)=-5.$ Since $1\\notin [-5,-1],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,0],$ we have $g(-1)=-5$ and $g(0)=2.$ Since $1\\in [-5,2],$ the IVT *does * guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[0,1],$ we have $g(0)=2$ and $g(1)=3.$ Since $1\\notin [2,3],$ the IVT does *not* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,2],$ we have $g(1)=3$ and $g(2)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-4,-3],$ $[-1,0],$  and $[1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least three solutions on $[-4,2].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345582 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345582 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345582 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a513028-7494-52ef-ad77-c476ad1110db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345941 #edb/ref 1080 "k=-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345941 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345941 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4467b80f-ae50-5444-a167-60efa89eaef4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346030 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346030 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346030 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4f653602-ed65-5294-8d6c-a6ef40f95a14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346175 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346175 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346175 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5f729d70-c219-5012-b6a4-33000a396e60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346303 #edb/ref 1080 "k=10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346303 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346303 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6d5511ff-7d5c-5a1f-845a-ce67bfc8d306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346453 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346453 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346453 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7de2999b-8a5b-506d-ad8d-ee40e381570c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346732 #edb/ref 1080 "k=5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346732 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346732 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9e66446d-348d-5a6a-9a83-40552ce32d71" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347047 #edb/ref 1080 "k=9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347047 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347047 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c463a5ea-6e6c-5c44-ae22-48ca75bf8431" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347069 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347069 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347069 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c5ab31d1-2bed-5320-91a1-aa82ac71d5a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347304 #edb/ref 1080 "k=-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347304 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347304 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "de1cdaea-8c75-5e83-b7fd-f2cf0701f75a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186345582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186346453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347608 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "01a7db5c-f3b0-55a6-bffd-8b68714c8c17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186345941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347983 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347304 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347983 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347983 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347983 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186346732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347983 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d5f8dae9-4d09-5215-9e12-2d4e29684707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348086 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348086 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348086 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348086 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=8-(x+2)^{2}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $x\\in [-2,1]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348086 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "03e4b68b-1dcd-579e-9183-2b2d2ce44e09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348086 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,1],$ so the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-2)$ and $f(1).$\n\nSince $f(-2)=8$ and $f(1)=-1,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-1,8].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=5$ belongs to the interval $[-1,8].$\n\n![](images/4b/4b9b0cecb305a49f98b7b7a6a2ebca69c1ce10acdb72cb88d805e0f1901dc882.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348092 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348092 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348092 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348092 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $g(x)$ is continuous over $x\\in [-1,2].$ Some values of $g(x)$ are given in the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $2$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nWhat is the smallest possible number of solutions to the equation $g(x)=1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348092 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae2fb37c-3d07-5853-b99d-9e361dcd461b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348092 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $g$ is a continuous function on $[-1,2]$, we can apply the intermediate value theorem (IVT) in any subinterval of $[-1,2].$\n\nWe will go through each subinterval of $[-1,2]$ and check if the IVT guarantees a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this interval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[-1,1],$ we have $g(-1)=2$ and $g(1)=0.$ Since $1\\in [0,2],$ the IVT *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\n- On the subinterval $[1,2],$ we have $g(1)=0$ and $g(2)=3.$ Since $1\\in [0,3],$ the IVT  *does* guarantee a solution to the equation $g(x)=1$ on this subinterval.\n\nThe IVT guarantees solutions to $g(x)=1$ on the subintervals $[-1,1]$ and $[1,2].$ Therefore, $g(x)=1$ has at least two solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186428672 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428672 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2150->1814. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428672 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186051049 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428673 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428673 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations